WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.429
FIPS 205 is the Stateless Hash-Based Digital Signature Standard.

2
00:00:04.429 --> 00:00:09.254
The standardized algorithm name is SLH-DSA, associated with SPHINCS+.

3
00:00:09.254 --> 00:00:14.870
At a high level, hash-based signatures use hash functions as their core foundation.

4
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:24.598
That gives SLH-DSA a different design basis from lattice-based signature schemes such as ML-DSA, which can be valuable for algorithm diversity.

5
00:00:24.598 --> 00:00:29.660
SLH-DSA is not necessarily the default answer for every signature use case.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.694
It has tradeoffs involving signature size and performance.

7
00:00:33.694 --> 00:00:45.796
Larger signatures can affect bandwidth, storage, certificate chains, embedded devices, update packages, and protocols that were designed around smaller classical signatures.

8
00:00:45.796 --> 00:00:52.202
Slower signing or verification may matter in systems that process large volumes of signatures.

9
00:00:52.202 --> 00:00:55.524
Those tradeoffs do not make SLH-DSA unimportant.

10
00:00:55.524 --> 00:01:03.829
Conservative design and diversity can be valuable when organizations want a backup option based on different assumptions.

11
00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:16.880
Some use cases may tolerate larger signatures because the number of signatures is low, the artifacts are important, or the operational environment values a different cryptographic foundation.

12
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.733
Security practitioners should understand SLH-DSA as part of the standards portfolio.

13
00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:30.405
It helps answer the question, what if we do not want every signature migration to depend on one mathematical family?

14
00:01:30.405 --> 00:01:40.529
The right choice still depends on system constraints, validation needs, performance, interoperability, and the risk model behind the signed object.
